WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.300
How far can counts stray from a ratio before chance becomes a poor explanation?

2
00:00:10.100 --> 00:00:12.660
Chapter 1 showed that real counts usually scatter.

3
00:00:13.940 --> 00:00:15.120
Now we measure the scatter.

4
00:00:16.200 --> 00:00:17.600
Back to Mendel's seeds.

5
00:00:18.980 --> 00:00:21.840
Every class missed its predicted count by a few seeds.

6
00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:25.240
Some came in high, and some came in low.

7
00:00:26.420 --> 00:00:28.280
Is that close enough to keep the ratio?

8
00:00:29.500 --> 00:00:31.520
a miss of a few seeds a lot?

9
00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:33.960
That depends on the class.

10
00:00:35.180 --> 00:00:38.900
In this cross, chance moves a bigger pile further than a smaller one.

11
00:00:40.100 --> 00:00:42.920
The round yellow pile, the biggest, strays furthest.

12
00:00:43.760 --> 00:00:46.280
The small wrinkled green pile strays least.

13
00:00:47.600 --> 00:00:50.480
So judge each miss against the scatter in its own class.

14
00:00:51.550 --> 00:00:53.650
Karl Pearson built a score for this.

15
00:00:54.890 --> 00:00:58.130
For each class, square the miss, so high and low both count.

16
00:00:59.770 --> 00:01:01.050
Divide by the expected count.

17
00:01:02.270 --> 00:01:04.990
That roughly puts each miss on its own class's scale.

18
00:01:06.310 --> 00:01:07.350
Then add the classes.

19
00:01:08.483 --> 00:01:10.183
The score is called chi-square.

20
00:01:11.663 --> 00:01:14.683
Zero means a perfect match, and bigger means further off.

21
00:01:16.103 --> 00:01:17.643
Mendel's seeds score well under one.

22
00:01:18.667 --> 00:01:19.927
But is that small?

23
00:01:21.107 --> 00:01:23.287
We need to know what chance alone produces.

24
00:01:24.507 --> 00:01:26.107
So assume the ratio is true.

25
00:01:27.347 --> 00:01:31.607
Let a computer grow Mendel's cross thousands of times, and score every run.

26
00:01:32.700 --> 00:01:36.440
Each run drops one dot on this line, at its chi-square score.

27
00:01:37.840 --> 00:01:39.380
Where will the pile of dots balance?

28
00:01:45.933 --> 00:01:48.053
It balances at three, not four.

29
00:01:49.333 --> 00:01:51.933
Each class adds a little less than one, on average.

30
00:01:53.253 --> 00:01:54.973
Four classes share one total.

31
00:01:56.253 --> 00:01:58.693
Once three counts are known, the fourth is fixed.

32
00:01:59.993 --> 00:02:01.653
So only three misses are free.

33
00:02:02.953 --> 00:02:04.633
Three is the degrees of freedom.

34
00:02:05.717 --> 00:02:07.197
Now mark the far edge.

35
00:02:08.537 --> 00:02:11.037
Only one run in twenty scores beyond this line.

36
00:02:12.477 --> 00:02:14.757
Counts out here would be rare if the ratio held.

37
00:02:15.837 --> 00:02:17.697
That is a reason to doubt the ratio.

38
00:02:21.733 --> 00:02:23.993
Now drop Mendel's score onto the pile.

39
00:02:25.233 --> 00:02:27.813
It sits far to the left, deep inside the pile.

40
00:02:29.113 --> 00:02:31.573
Most chance runs land further out than his did.

41
00:02:32.773 --> 00:02:34.873
That share of runs is called the p-value.

42
00:02:36.017 --> 00:02:37.117
Read it carefully.

43
00:02:38.557 --> 00:02:43.257
The p-value is worked out as if the ratio, and every other assumption, were true.

44
00:02:44.417 --> 00:02:48.397
That means independent seeds, correct scoring and equal survival.

45
00:02:49.737 --> 00:02:51.917
It is not the chance that the ratio is true.

46
00:02:52.967 --> 00:02:55.847
And a large p-value does not prove the ratio.

47
00:02:57.147 --> 00:02:59.347
It gives no reason to reject the ratio.

48
00:03:00.687 --> 00:03:03.087
Other models might fit these counts just as well.

49
00:03:04.117 --> 00:03:05.617
So here is the idea.

50
00:03:06.937 --> 00:03:08.757
A ratio predicts a spread of counts.

51
00:03:10.017 --> 00:03:13.817
If the ratio holds, chi-square averages its degrees of freedom.

52
00:03:15.097 --> 00:03:18.237
Far above that, doubt the ratio, but never call it proof.
